{
 "item": {
  "title": "Millcreek terrain and slope",
  "type": "Web Map",
  "snippet": "Slope classes, 2 m contours and hillshade from the 0.5 m lidar terrain. Unvalidated LiDAR planning estimates from the 2023 Salt Lake Valley QL1 lidar (flown October-November 2023); not surveyed or field-verified data.",
  "description": "<p><b>Unvalidated LiDAR planning estimates from the 2023 Salt Lake Valley QL1 lidar (flown October-November 2023); not surveyed or field-verified data.</b></p><p>Slope in six classes from the 0.5 m bare-earth terrain (3 m neighbourhood, 4 m² minimum mapping unit), shaded by the lidar hillshade blended over it (multiply), with 2 m contours (10 m index lines heavier) from 1:288,000 inward and the 2 m lines from 1:18,000.</p><p><b>Slope classes:</b> <span style='color:#FFFFCC'>■</span> Under 10% &nbsp; <span style='color:#FED976'>■</span> 10% to 15% &nbsp; <span style='color:#FEB24C'>■</span> 15% to 20% &nbsp; <span style='color:#FD8D3C'>■</span> 20% to 25% &nbsp; <span style='color:#F03B20'>■</span> 25% to 30% &nbsp; <span style='color:#BD0026'>■</span> 30% and over</p><p><b>Contours:</b> <span style='color:#3D2610'>▬</span> 10 m index (heavy) &nbsp; <span style='color:#5A3A1A'>─</span> 2 m (thin), elevations in metres (NAVD88); the tiles carry no elevation values, so the lines are unlabelled</p><p>Slope and contours are vector tiles: they draw but have no pop-ups, and the map legend cannot list them (the key above stands in).</p><p>Datum: NAD83(2011) taken to WGS84 / Web Mercator with WGS_1984_(ITRF08)_To_NAD_1983_2011, the same for every layer. Records: canopy-toolbox/reviews/2026-10-23/agol-styling in buschbrian/agol-python-scripts.</p>",
